#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487454} :activity/id #uuid "53942e05-26a9-5828-9055-a80f3d69fad5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048441 :topic/id #uuid "9faf7c3f-8d4f-5e48-97ed-6c10e2c5383a" :topic/math-academy-id 291} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487413 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234376 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b25b7d1-2205-5d45-9eb4-1153c39f2254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486678} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf1ee9d-fb31-555f-8234-c02cee50db01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486946} {:answer/id #uuid "cd994e00-b7e4-5872-9698-8e0bb1e9c264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487046} {:answer/id #uuid "f42704d7-ba6a-5ac9-b641-e73d8c7eb55c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487116} {:answer/id #uuid "f70d98a5-70c4-581c-95be-04705131018f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487046} :answer-field/id #uuid "401d966c-98a8-5ffc-958c-1d19edfa6b7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2576954f-b0ee-571c-85ae-5533e6d22543" :question/math-academy-id "q-75836" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $5$ | $4$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=8$ and ${f}^{′}(8)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(8)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "29ee00b7-02cb-5105-a7be-7bc30258a733" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487421 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317292 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2be8b47e-8161-5ac6-af15-f112a84dd1b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315375} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdde61d2-0b02-5fd6-a68f-661c818e9a55" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "53ed59d8-b22e-538d-9cc1-0f9210effe07" :question/math-academy-id "q-342596" :question/problem "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=1,$ $g(2)=-1,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2},$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2)+\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{(-1)}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(-1)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "410fa10e-cbe7-5086-9bf0-1df3c0ba90fb"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486740} {:answer/id #uuid "59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486791} {:answer/id #uuid "64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486815} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} {:answer/id #uuid "c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "10dcb0f4-5f0f-5b93-a598-6c0cd2ed4435" :question/math-academy-id "q-76105" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "53aab599-8ea7-5095-9e1f-104bd68f1330" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9f522c8-2e41-549d-a3cd-753ef997b9f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487000}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487000} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd876f7-79e5-597c-ac7b-34096cd74827" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f03606b0-4d82-587c-8251-04c53399de85" :question/math-academy-id "q-342572" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b1735679cbf165e26ac22f1fd3076d450231ea8c20c1905ec74f008bc89ec33a.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,1)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=1.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $-1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "660230ab-1276-560d-bdd5-765d8d1b7e46" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487421}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a9914a1-bd63-5503-bb36-c2533c2c5218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063355} {:answer/id #uuid "8cc67312-c83d-5cab-b917-ee2161d29129" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063356} {:answer/id #uuid "c283d4fd-1d67-5c92-a428-3fc3d98830e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063357} {:answer/id #uuid "cad706a5-f7c0-5286-9b96-bf0ba4a525c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063358} {:answer/id #uuid "de134615-356c-50aa-8b5e-53d741c02ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063356} :answer-field/id #uuid "5881a3d1-5740-518f-bfdd-3ac99c905e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59090c66-6332-5a45-a658-fc8034750491" :question/math-academy-id "q-5028" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and the point $P(0,0)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$  calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0).$" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,0).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,0)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(0,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f5586187-a46c-585d-9b8d-9f88bb39ba76" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487413}}] :activity/title "Differentiating an Inverse Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486589}]}
